[49.738717, "o", "\r\n[approval] bash: python3 -m ensurepip --uninstall 2>&1; echo ---; ls /usr/local/lib/python3.13/site-packages/; echo ---; python3 -m ensurepip 2>&1; echo ---; python3 -m pip --version 2>&1 — approved\r\n  ⚙ bash python3 -m ensurepip --uninstall 2>&1; echo ---; ls /usr/local/lib/pyt…\r\n    ✗ bash — exit 1\r\n  qwen3.8-27b — 6 tool call(s) so far\r\n[approval] bash: rm -rf /usr/local/lib/python3.13/site-packages/pip-24.3.1.dist-info; python3 -m ensurepip 2>&1; echo ---; python3 -m pip --version 2>&1; pip --version 2>&1 — approved\r\n  ⚙ bash rm -rf /usr/local/lib/python3.13/site-packages/pip-24.3.1.dist-info; p…\r\n    ✓ bash — exit 0\r\n  qwen3.8-27b — 7 tool call(s) so far\r\n[approval] bash: pip install --dry-run requests 2>&1 | tail -5 — approved\r\n  ⚙ bash pip install --dry-run requests 2>&1 | tail -5\r\n    ✓ bash — exit 0\r\n  qwen3.8-27b — 8 tool call(s) so far\r\nFixed. Here's what was wrong and what I did:\r\n\r\n**Diagnosis:** `pip` and `pip3` both failed with `ModuleNotFoundError: No module"]
[49.738724, "o", " named 'pip'`. The cause: `/usr/local/lib/python3.13/site-packages/` contained only a stale `pip-24.3.1.dist-info` directory (metadata claiming pip was installed) but the actual `pip/` package files were missing. Because of that leftover dist-info, `python3 -m ensurepip` reported \"Requirement already satisfied\" and refused to reinstall.\r\n\r\n**Fix:**\r\n1. Removed the stale `pip-24.3.1.dist-info` directory.\r\n2. Ran `python3 -m ensurepip`, which reinstalled pip 24.3.1 from its bundled wheel.\r\n\r\n**Verification:**\r\n- `pip --version` → `pip 24.3.1 from /usr/local/lib/python3.13/site-packages/pip (python 3.13)`\r\n- `pip install --dry-run requests` → resolves and downloads fine, so installing packages works again.\r\n\r\nOne side note: pip now suggests upgrading to 26.3 (`pip install --upgrade pip`) — optional, up to you.\r\n\r\n[result] role=implementor model=qwen3.8-27b tools=8 surface=47bfe6dff29f\r\n"]
